WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.881
A certificate is a statement that binds a name or identity to a public key
under a trust model.

2
00:00:04.881 --> 00:00:09.256
The matching private key is held by the service or subject that owns that
identity.

3
00:00:09.256 --> 00:00:13.884
When a Python client connects to a remote service, the service presents a
certificate.

4
00:00:13.884 --> 00:00:21.047
The client then needs to decide whether the certificate is valid for the
service being contacted and for the purpose of the connection.

5
00:00:21.047 --> 00:00:24.027
Certificate authorities make that decision scalable.

6
00:00:24.027 --> 00:00:26.499
A root certificate represents a trust anchor.

7
00:00:26.499 --> 00:00:30.810
Intermediate certificates connect end-entity certificates back to that
root.

8
00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:37.911
The certificate chain is the sequence that lets a client determine whether
an endpoint certificate is linked to a trusted authority.

9
00:00:37.911 --> 00:00:42.665
The trust store is the set of roots or trust anchors the client accepts
during validation.

10
00:00:42.665 --> 00:00:48.434
If the chain does not lead to a trusted anchor, the client should not
treat the endpoint as trusted by default.

11
00:00:48.434 --> 00:00:49.892
Names and purposes matter.

12
00:00:49.892 --> 00:00:56.549
Certificates may include subject names and subject alternative names that
identify the service or identity at a high level.

13
00:00:56.549 --> 00:01:03.332
Certificates may also include intended purposes or key usage concepts that
describe what the certificate is meant to support.

14
00:01:03.332 --> 00:01:07.833
Expiration dates define the window in which the certificate is expected to
be valid.

15
00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:13.793
Trust depends on the chain, the name, the purpose, and timing, not simply
on the existence of a certificate file.

16
00:01:13.793 --> 00:01:18.040
Private key exposure can undermine the identity represented by a
certificate.

17
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:25.648
If an attacker or unauthorized party obtains the private key, they may be
able to impersonate or sign for that identity depending on the system.

18
00:01:25.648 --> 00:01:33.509
Private keys should not appear in repositories, logs, support bundles,
broadly shared file systems, container images, or casual documentation.

19
00:01:33.509 --> 00:01:38.200
Certificate validation relies on the assumption that the private key
remains private.
